{"response": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "Airports can actually be great places to catch up on things, relax, or just have a little fun while you wait. Here are some of the best ways to kill time at the airport, depending on your mood:\n\n**1. Entertain Yourself**\n*   **Download and Binge:** Take advantage of the airport Wi-Fi (or your pre-downloads) to binge-watch a show, watch a long movie, or listen to an audiobook/podcast. \n*   **People-Watch:** Airports are arguably the best places in the world for people-watching. Grab a coffee, find a seat facing the concourse, and make up backstories for the people walking by.\n*   **Browse the Shops:** Even if you don\u2019t buy anything, wandering through duty-free shops, browsing the bookstores, or looking at high-end boutiques can easily kill an hour.\n*   **Play Games:** Whether it's a mobile game on your phone, a Nintendo Switch, or a deck of cards if you are traveling with a companion.\n\n**2. Be Productive**\n*   **Digital Declutter:** Delete old photos from your phone, clear out your email inbox, or organize your apps. It\u2019s a mindless but highly satisfying task.\n*   **Plan Your Trip:** If you are heading on a vacation, use this time to research restaurants, map out sightseeing routes, or read up on the history of your destination.\n*   **Catch Up on Work or Life:** Bring your laptop and knock out some work, or use the time to call a friend or family member you haven\u2019t spoken to in a while.\n\n**3. Relax and Recharge**\n*   **Buy a Lounge Pass:** Many airport lounges sell day passes (usually between $30 and $60). They offer free food, drinks, fast Wi-Fi, comfortable seating, and a much quieter atmosphere. \n*   **Get a Massage:** Many major airports have quick-service spas (like XpresSpa) where you can get a 15-to-30-minute chair massage or a manicure.\n*   **Read:** Hit the airport bookstore, grab a bestseller or a magazine, and find an empty gate to read in peace.\n\n**4. Get Moving**\n*   **Get Your Steps In:** Sitting on a plane is cramped. Use your waiting time to walk from one end of the terminal to the other. \n*   **Find a Yoga Room:** Some modern airports (like San Francisco, Chicago O'Hare, and Dallas/Fort Worth) actually have dedicated yoga rooms where you can stretch and meditate.\n\n**5. Treat Yourself**\n*   **Have a \"Real\" Meal:** Instead of grabbing fast food, find a sit-down restaurant in the terminal. Sit at the bar, order a nice meal, have a drink, and chat with the bartender or fellow travelers. \n\n*Pro-tip: No matter what you do, set an alarm on your phone for 15 minutes before your flight begins boarding so you don't lose track of time!*", "thoughtSignature": ""}], "role": "model"}, "finishReason": "STOP", "index": 0}], "usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 38, "candidatesTokenCount": 644, "totalTokenCount": 1280, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 38}], "thoughtsTokenCount": 598, "serviceTier": "standard"}, "modelVersion": "gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "responseId": "ABnHapzcLeP4qtsPhpeBkAY"}, "metadata": {"key": "b770fec8afdc74c1b30f271d2b9d77f99cad534605aeaf179b6c83ba8e355cd7"}}
